<!--
# Hello Mail

A self-hosted mail server for two domains: one EC2 box running Stalwart, its
metadata in RDS Postgres and its message bodies in S3, with every outbound
message relayed through Amazon SES so delivery never rides one IP's reputation.

The point of the example is the pair of `<MailDomainBlock/>`s. A mail domain is
declared once, as the domain it serves, and everything composes from it: the SES
sending identity, its three DKIM selectors, the `MX`/`SPF`/`DMARC`/`MTA-STS`
records that make it deliverable, the `SRV` and autoconfig records that make it
discoverable, and the mailboxes it holds. Serving a second domain is a second
tag, which is exactly what the second one here is.

The two are deliberately unalike, because that is what a second domain is for:
`example.com` holds people's mailboxes, and `news.example.com` sends
publications and welcomes replies into a catch-all. They share the box and the
zone and nothing else, so a newsletter that burns its reputation cannot take
anyone's mail down with it.

## Usage

    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx validate
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx plan
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx deploy
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx destroy --force

`validate` needs only `tofu`. Live deploy needs AWS credentials, a Cloudflare
api token in `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`, and `aws`/`ssh`/`curl` on the deploy
machine. It also needs domains you actually own: every name below is an RFC 2606
example domain and the zone id is a placeholder, so a real deploy replaces both.

Note the zone is written out on all three blocks (`dns={Cloudflare{..}}`) only
so the example validates with nothing configured, which is also why it repeats.
A real stack omits `dns` entirely and sets `CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID`: which zone a
launch writes into is a property of the launch rather than of any declaration,
so each block reads it from the environment and none of them restate it.

The box needs a zone of its own because the record it publishes is its own: an
`A` at `mail.example.com`, which every `MX` here targets and which its
certificate and reverse record are issued against. The box is infrastructure,
not a mail domain, so it owns that name rather than borrowing one.

## The deploy order, and why it is that

`<EnsureSecret/>` hands its generated value to the apply as a request param, so
it sits in this route and ahead of `<TofuApply/>`: the database is created WITH
its master password, and the box boots reading its admin credential out of the
parameter the same step minted. Neither value appears here or in any log.

Then the reverse record (AWS validates the forward one first, so it waits for
dns), then `<StalwartProvision/>`. That last one is not a nicety: Stalwart 0.16
keeps its listeners, its routing, its domains and its accounts as objects INSIDE
its own data store, so a freshly built box serves nothing until they are applied
over its management api. Terraform owns the machine; provision owns the mail
server. Finally `<MailProbe/>` sends a message out through the whole stack and
reads one back in, asserting the receiving end believes both.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<CrateCheck features={["infra", "extra"]}/>
	<!-- the region is a DECISION here (an mta near its users, a database near
	     its mta), so the stack pins it rather than inheriting `AWS_REGION`: a
	     deploy from a differently-configured machine builds the same stack. -->
	<Stack app_name="mail-example" region="us-west-2">
		<DeployRoutes/>

		<!-- the network the box sits in. No NAT gateway: the box has a public
		     address of its own and the database has nothing to call out to. -->
		<VpcBlock bx:ref="net" label="net"/>
		<!-- mail metadata, in the private subnets, reachable only from the
		     security groups of the consumers whose `DatabaseRef` targets it
		     (the box below; each consumer emits its own admission). -->
		<RdsPostgresBlock bx:ref="db" label="db" database="mail" {VpcRef($net)}/>
		<!-- message bodies: the source of record, so versioned and not
		     force-destroyable. -->
		<S3BucketBlock label="mail-blobs" deploy_versioned=false force_destroy=false
			runtime_write=true object_versioning=true/>
		<!-- the `archive` profile: cold, append-only, written by the box rather
		     than by any deploy. The nightly dump under `postgres/` is a copy of a
		     live database and expires after a season; a prefix holding the only
		     copy of something would sit beside it and expire never, which is why
		     the expiry is scoped to a prefix rather than to the bucket. -->
		<S3BucketBlock label="archive" deploy_versioned=false runtime_write=true
			object_versioning=true
			expire_prefixes={[{prefix:"postgres/", expire_days:180}]}/>

		<!-- the box, which is infrastructure rather than a mail domain: its name,
		     its reverse record, its certificate and its SMTP banner stay put while
		     the domains it serves come and go. It holds no state worth keeping, so
		     any machine-config change simply replaces it. -->
		<StalwartBlock label="mail" hostname="mail.example.com"
			blob_bucket="mail-blobs" backup_bucket="archive"
			{(VpcRef($net), DatabaseRef($db))}
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"mail.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			ssh_public_key="ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam you@example.com"/>

		<!-- the domain people have mailboxes on. `postmaster@` and `abuse@` are the
		     two addresses an outside party actually tries when something is wrong,
		     so a served domain answers them from day one. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			mailboxes={[
				{localpart:"hello", admin:true},
				{localpart:"probe"},
			]}
			aliases={[
				{localpart:"postmaster", target:"hello"},
				{localpart:"abuse", target:"hello"},
			]}/>

		<!-- the publication domain: its own identity and its own reputation, with
		     replies welcomed into a catch-all (a newsletter that bounces its
		     replies reads as unattended). Its reports go to the domain whose
		     mailboxes are read by a human. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="news.example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			report_domain="example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"news.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			catch_all="replies"
			mailboxes={[{localpart:"replies"}]}/>

		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<EnsureSecret secret="db-password" variable="db_password"/>
			<EnsureSecret secret="mail-admin-password"/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<EipReverseDns/>
			<StalwartProvision/>
			<MailProbe mailbox="probe" sender_domain="news.example.com"/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
